On 2003.06.15 03:49, Chris Hecker wrote:
> Yeah, I searched and found that right after posting, thanks.  But,=20
> from reading about it on various list archives, it seems unclear=20
> whether you can just install 3.2 out of unstable without it replacing=20
> 2.95.

Yes, you can install them side by side.  The default compiler (invoked=20
with 'cc', 'gcc', etc.) will remain 2.95 until you upgrade to version=20
of package gcc-default that changes it.  You invoke 3.2 with 'gcc-3.2';=20
you will need to arrange for your kernel compilation to use this=20
compiler (perhaps as simple as 'export CC=3D"gcc-3.2"').  I haven't done=20
this in a while.  Even after you transition, you can keep 2.9.5 around=20
using a similar mechanism.

BTW, the gcc 3.x ABI transition does not affect straight C code (like=20
the kernel) at all.  That part of the calling standard has not=20
changed.  What's affected is any C++ libraries.  It's name mangling=20
that changed.  By all accounts the gcc transition is majorly good news=20
for Alpha, due to (a) fixed code generation bugs and (b) overall better=20
code quality for RISC.

-Doug



